<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cortenuova</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1237">1237</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cortenuova was fought on 27 November 1237 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, when the emperor Frederick II defeated the army of the revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The Milanese carroccio, the city's battle-wagon, was captured and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a trophy. The emperor's demand for unconditional surrender stiffened the cities' resistance, however, and he never broke the league.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cortenuova.jpg" alt="Battle of Cortenuova" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Villani, Nuova Cronica, Chronica, 14. Jahrhundert, Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Rom Cod. Chigi L VIII 296, fol. 76v. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erbeutung_des_Mail%C3%A4nder_Carroccios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1235 Frederick II resolved to renew the struggle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> communes, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> supported diplomatically; the dispute turned on how far the rights recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-constance">Peace of Constance</a> could coexist with imperial jurisdiction. In 1236, helped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano</a>, he stormed and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>. He returned in September 1237 with around 2,000 German knights, joined by troops from Tuscany and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, among them Muslim archers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Lucera</a>, and contingents from Cremona, Pavia, Parma and other allies. Mantua submitted, and Montichiari surrendered on 21 October after almost three weeks.</p><p>The League army, probably about 15,000 men under Pietro Tiepolo, podestà of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and son of the Venetian doge, held a position near Manerbio protected by rivers, marshes and autumn rain. After weeks of stalemate Frederick began withdrawing across the Oglio on 23 November, keeping a mobile force at Soncino, and the League set off for winter quarters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In heavy rain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> column became stretched out, its baggage several kilometres behind. Bergamese patrols shadowed it, and smoke signals passed from Cividate al Piano through a chain of towers to Soncino. Imperial propaganda later presented the operation as a planned trap, though Frederick's own early account called the opportunity accidental. His troops fell first on the rearguard, which resisted for about an hour. Tiepolo then formed a line near Cortenuova, the carroccio at its centre, infantry in close ranks behind shields and spears, protected by a shallow ditch.</p><p>Frederick, with probably more than 10,000 men, combined Saracen archery, Cremonese infantry with long axes and repeated cavalry charges. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> cavalry counterattack broke into his position, but the infantry was pressed back to the carroccio, and darkness ended the fighting. In the night retreat towards Treviglio the army fell apart; men drowned crossing the swollen Serio or were captured, and Tiepolo was taken. Contemporary estimates of the League's losses differ widely, from a Milanese tradition of at least 500 cavalry and 2,000 infantry killed or captured among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>'s forces alone to imperial claims of about 10,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Frederick entered Cremona with the carroccio drawn by his elephant and Tiepolo in chains upon it; Tiepolo was later executed at Trani. The carroccio was displayed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> with an inscription celebrating Caesar's triumph, a spectacle that humiliated Gregory IX in his own city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, Vercelli and Novara submitted, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> refused unconditional surrender, Frederick's siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> failed in 1238, and his renewed excommunication in 1239 followed from the altered balance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Cortenuova” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The league was revived in 1226 against Frederick II, who defeated it at <b>Cortenuova</b> in 1237 but failed to break the cities.</blockquote><a href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a></li></ul></section>
